  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1038 PM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nA deep trough off the West Coast and High pressure to the southeast \nnear Big Bend is keeping the Desert Southwest under a strong south \nto southwesterly flow aloft advecting deep subtropical moisture \nnorth through Arizona into eastern Utah and Western Colorado. Models \nare in good agreement with satellite imagery showing the low just \nturning the corner in the base of the trough off the SoCal Coast \nbefore ejecting to the northeast into the Great Basin Thursday \nevening and continuing into Montana Friday evening taking most of \nthe moisture with it. Meanwhile, another shortwave system descending \ninto the trough Friday cuts off over SoCal Friday night to eject \nfarther east over the region Saturday night into Sunday to keep \nunsettled weather in the forecast through the weekend. Models stay \nin fairly good agreement through the weekend, but are diverging on \nthe timing and track of the second system by Sunday with some \nsolutions tracking it to the west over northern Utah. The models do \ngenerally agree on setting up a dry zonal flow across the region \nnext week that could extend into the next weekend, but that a long \nway out and confidence is lot on the extended period. \n\nToday we saw quieter showers with rain rates remaining on the low \nside only around a half inch per hour in the heavier showers. Even \nthough we had strong vertical sheer and plenty moisture on hand, \nwith all the heavy cloud cover and the warm air aloft, we just \ndidn't have any CAPE to feed stronger storms. Both the 18Z and 00Z \nsoundings showed a saturated atmosphere nearly up to 200mb for \nefficient rain making, but with zero CAPE there wasn't the strong \nconvection. That being said, much of the southern half of the region \nstill received an inch or more rain today saturating the soil with \nwater and priming the area for flash flooding should heavier showers \ncome overnight into tomorrow. \n\nThese conditions look to change overnight into tomorrow as we come \nmore under the influence of the trough to the west cooling the air \naloft to provide some CAPE to support convection. Additionally, upper-\nlevel dynamics increase as a 70-90 kt jet moves in over the region. \nThe primary concern with convection will be strong winds over 60 mph \npossible as the winds aloft mix down. The atmosphere remains \nsaturated through the day, making localized heavy rain in showers \nand thunderstorms much more likely leading to flash flooding, \nespecially near recent fire scars and areas prone to flooding like \nslot canyons and steep terrain. Stay tuned to the forecast for \nupdates as weather develops through the day. Drier air starts moving \ninto the region late tomorrow which looks to scatter out the showers \nand thunderstorms, but also looks to increase the potential for \nstronger thunderstorms overnight into Friday. Most of the moisture \nis swept out of the region Friday limiting convective activity \nthrough the afternoon, but has the second low starts moving in from \nthe west, showers and thunderstorms will pick up again Friday night \ninto Saturday feeding on the residual moisture and continue through \nSaturday night and Sunday as the low tracks over the region. The \nstorms move on off to the east as a zonal pattern sets up across the \nregion Sunday night through mid next week.",
